export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"addon":"hvac","name":"HVAC","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"hvac-start","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: getting started","summary":"What the HVAC add-on does in CadCraft, how to switch it on, and how ductwork is kept in an ordinary DWG or DXF file.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["hvac","add-on","ductwork","duct","mechanical","sheet metal","airflow","cfm","equal friction","schedule"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-duct-run","text":"Your first duct run","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHVAC is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It draws ductwork: you click the centerline of a duct run, and the add-on draws both sides of the duct with its elbows, transitions and branch fittings, sizes it from the airflow, builds it as a solid on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab and lists it in a duct schedule with its sheet metal area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHVAC\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHVAC\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHVAC\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESizes follow the drawing&#039;s units: a drawing in inches or feet gets sizes in inches, airflow in cfm and friction in inches of water per 100 feet; any other drawing gets millimeters, liters per second and pascals per meter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-duct-run\"\u003EYour first duct run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCT\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E and a size, for example \u003Ccode\u003E24x12\u003C/code\u003E (width × height) — or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E and the airflow, and the add-on picks the size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the points of the run. Every corner gets an elbow by itself. Press Enter to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo branch off, start the next run inside the first one: the click moves onto its centerline and the branch gets a tee or a takeoff there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTSCHED\u003C/code\u003E and click where the schedule goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:hvac/hvac-ducts\" data-topic=\"addon:hvac/hvac-ducts\"\u003EDrawing duct runs\u003C/a\u003E for everything a run can do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA duct run is saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name \u003Ccode\u003ECC_HVAC\u003C/code\u003E. So ductwork saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as the drawn duct, and opens in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. Its solids are ordinary 3D groups on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab. With HVAC switched on, the runs are duct runs again.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is HVAC is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It draws ductwork: you click the centerline of a duct run, and the add-on draws both sides of the duct with its elbows, transitions and branch fittings, sizes it from the airflow, builds it as a solid on the 3D tab and lists it in a duct schedule with its sheet metal area. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › HVAC. Its tools are in the HVAC section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › HVAC, as a group at the end of the toolbar. Sizes follow the drawing's units: a drawing in inches or feet gets sizes in inches, airflow in cfm and friction in inches of water per 100 feet; any other drawing gets millimeters, liters per second and pascals per meter. Your first duct run Type DUCT. Type Z and a size, for example 24x12 (width × height) — or A and the airflow, and the add-on picks the size. Click the points of the run. Every corner gets an elbow by itself. Press Enter to finish. To branch off, start the next run inside the first one: the click moves onto its centerline and the branch gets a tee or a takeoff there. Type DUCTSCHED and click where the schedule goes. See Drawing duct runs for everything a run can do. Files open anywhere A duct run is saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name CC_HVAC. So ductwork saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as the drawn duct, and opens in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. Its solids are ordinary 3D groups on the 3D tab. With HVAC switched on, the runs are duct runs again."},{"slug":"hvac-ducts","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: drawing duct runs","summary":"Draw rectangular, round and flat oval duct runs on their centerline; elbows, transitions, tees, takeoffs and laterals come by themselves.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["duct","run","centerline","elbow","radius elbow","mitered elbow","transition","reducer","tee","takeoff","lateral","branch","bottom of duct","elevation"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-a-run","text":"Drawing a run","level":2},{"id":"the-fittings-it-makes","text":"The fittings it makes","level":2},{"id":"changing-a-run","text":"Changing a run","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-a-run\"\u003EDrawing a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCT\u003C/code\u003E and click the points of the run&#039;s centerline. While you click, these options change what comes next:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShape\u003C/span\u003E — rectangular, round or flat oval (a flat oval lies with its flat sides top and bottom).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E — width × height (\u003Ccode\u003E24x12\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E600x300\u003C/code\u003E) or a diameter (\u003Ccode\u003E14\u003C/code\u003E). The next sections get it; a size change makes a transition.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAirflow\u003C/span\u003E — the section&#039;s airflow; the add-on sizes it by equal friction (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:hvac/hvac-sizing\" data-topic=\"addon:hvac/hvac-sizing\"\u003ESizing ducts\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E — the bottom of the duct, before the first point (9 ft or 2700 mm unless you change it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESystem\u003C/span\u003E — supply, return, exhaust, outside air or other. Each has its own layer (\u003Ccode\u003EM-HVAC-SUPP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM-HVAC-RETN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM-HVAC-EXHS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM-HVAC-OAIR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM-HVAC-DUCT\u003C/code\u003E) and its own color on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E — a name such as \u003Ccode\u003ES-1\u003C/code\u003E, used by the schedule and the 3D tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E — takes the last point back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run holds up to 100 points; start another run from its end for more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-fittings-it-makes\"\u003EThe fittings it makes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElbows\u003C/strong\u003E on every turn. A radius elbow&#039;s centerline radius is the duct&#039;s width times the run&#039;s ratio (1 for rectangular, 1.5 for round and flat oval, as is usual; change it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E). A mitered elbow has a square throat and a miter line; choose it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETransitions\u003C/strong\u003E where the size or shape changes, just after the corner (after its elbow). Each side slopes at most 15° (a default the add-on uses, not a code value), and none is shorter than 6 in or 150 mm. Round to round is called a reducer. The bottom stays flat; the top steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBranches\u003C/strong\u003E: start a run inside another, or end it inside one, and the point moves onto that run&#039;s centerline. The branch is cut back to the main&#039;s side, gets a 2 in or 50 mm collar, and the main gets an opening there. A round or flat oval branch is a takeoff; a rectangular one is a tee when it meets the main square (within 15°) and a lateral otherwise.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changing-a-run\"\u003EChanging a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a point&#039;s grip to move it; the fittings follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuct run\u003C/span\u003E: tag, system, shape, size (the whole run), bottom of duct, elbows, the radius ratio, material and size labels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTSIZE\u003C/code\u003E: pick a section, then size it from its airflow, type a size, or change its shape — for that section or the whole run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E work as on any object. A copy gets its own identity. A branch whose main moves away is no longer connected: draw it to the main again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Drawing a run Type DUCT and click the points of the run's centerline. While you click, these options change what comes next: Shape — rectangular, round or flat oval (a flat oval lies with its flat sides top and bottom). Size — width × height (24x12, 600x300) or a diameter (14). The next sections get it; a size change makes a transition. Airflow — the section's airflow; the add-on sizes it by equal friction (see Sizing ducts). Elevation — the bottom of the duct, before the first point (9 ft or 2700 mm unless you change it in Settings). System — supply, return, exhaust, outside air or other. Each has its own layer (M-HVAC-SUPP, M-HVAC-RETN, M-HVAC-EXHS, M-HVAC-OAIR, M-HVAC-DUCT) and its own color on the 3D tab. Tag — a name such as S-1, used by the schedule and the 3D tab. Undo — takes the last point back. A run holds up to 100 points; start another run from its end for more. The fittings it makes Elbows on every turn. A radius elbow's centerline radius is the duct's width times the run's ratio (1 for rectangular, 1.5 for round and flat oval, as is usual; change it in Properties). A mitered elbow has a square throat and a miter line; choose it in Settings or Properties. Transitions where the size or shape changes, just after the corner (after its elbow). Each side slopes at most 15° (a default the add-on uses, not a code value), and none is shorter than 6 in or 150 mm. Round to round is called a reducer. The bottom stays flat; the top steps. Branches: start a run inside another, or end it inside one, and the point moves onto that run's centerline. The branch is cut back to the main's side, gets a 2 in or 50 mm collar, and the main gets an opening there. A round or flat oval branch is a takeoff; a rectangular one is a tee when it meets the main square (within 15°) and a lateral otherwise. Changing a run Drag a point's grip to move it; the fittings follow. Properties › Duct run: tag, system, shape, size (the whole run), bottom of duct, elbows, the radius ratio, material and size labels. DUCTSIZE: pick a section, then size it from its airflow, type a size, or change its shape — for that section or the whole run. MOVE, ROTATE, MIRROR, COPY and ERASE work as on any object. A copy gets its own identity. A branch whose main moves away is no longer connected: draw it to the main again."},{"slug":"hvac-schedule","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: the duct schedule","summary":"Straight duct by size and material, every fitting, and the sheet metal area of each, as a table in the drawing or a CSV file.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["schedule","duct schedule","sheet metal","surface area","square feet","fittings","csv","quantities","length"],"headings":[{"id":"a-table-in-the-drawing","text":"A table in the drawing","level":2},{"id":"how-the-sheet-metal-is-worked-out","text":"How the sheet metal is worked out","level":2},{"id":"a-csv-file","text":"A CSV file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"a-table-in-the-drawing\"\u003EA table in the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTSCHED\u003C/code\u003E. The command line gives the totals; click where the table goes (its top left corner). The table lists:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Estraight duct\u003C/strong\u003E by shape, size and material, with its length and sheet metal area;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Efittings\u003C/strong\u003E — elbows, transitions and reducers, tees, takeoffs and laterals — by kind and size, with how many and their sheet metal;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea total.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table keeps itself up to date: every change to a duct run changes it in the same step (and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E takes both back). Its text height is set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHVAC\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-sheet-metal-is-worked-out\"\u003EHow the sheet metal is worked out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStraight duct: its perimeter × its length. A 24 × 12 in duct 10 ft long has 6 ft × 10 ft = 60 ft².\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElbows: the perimeter × the centerline length. For a radius elbow this is exact: the two cheeks, the heel and the throat add up to just that. A 90° 24 × 12 in elbow with a 24 in radius has 18.85 ft².\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERectangular transitions: their four flat sides, each worked out exactly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERound and mixed-shape transitions: the average perimeter × the slant length (exact for a round reducer centered on both ends, close for the others).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA branch&#039;s takeoff or tee: its collar, the branch&#039;s perimeter × 2 in (50 mm).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESeams, laps and waste are not included: add them in TakeoffCraft as waste, or by your shop&#039;s own rule.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-csv-file\"\u003EA CSV file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuct schedule (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E writes three tables: the straight duct (shape, size, material, length, perimeter, area), the fittings (count, area each, area), and every section (tag, system, size, length, airflow, velocity, friction).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo estimate from the schedule, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:hvac/hvac-takeoff\" data-topic=\"addon:hvac/hvac-takeoff\"\u003EDucts in TakeoffCraft\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A table in the drawing Type DUCTSCHED. The command line gives the totals; click where the table goes (its top left corner). The table lists: straight duct by shape, size and material, with its length and sheet metal area; fittings — elbows, transitions and reducers, tees, takeoffs and laterals — by kind and size, with how many and their sheet metal; a total. The table keeps itself up to date: every change to a duct run changes it in the same step (and UNDO takes both back). Its text height is set in Settings › CadCraft › HVAC. How the sheet metal is worked out Straight duct: its perimeter × its length. A 24 × 12 in duct 10 ft long has 6 ft × 10 ft = 60 ft². Elbows: the perimeter × the centerline length. For a radius elbow this is exact: the two cheeks, the heel and the throat add up to just that. A 90° 24 × 12 in elbow with a 24 in radius has 18.85 ft². Rectangular transitions: their four flat sides, each worked out exactly. Round and mixed-shape transitions: the average perimeter × the slant length (exact for a round reducer centered on both ends, close for the others). A branch's takeoff or tee: its collar, the branch's perimeter × 2 in (50 mm). Seams, laps and waste are not included: add them in TakeoffCraft as waste, or by your shop's own rule. A CSV file EXPORT › Duct schedule (CSV) writes three tables: the straight duct (shape, size, material, length, perimeter, area), the fittings (count, area each, area), and every section (tag, system, size, length, airflow, velocity, friction). To estimate from the schedule, see Ducts in TakeoffCraft."},{"slug":"hvac-3d","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: ducts on the 3D tab","summary":"Every duct run is also a solid on CadCraft's 3D tab, built from the same run and kept up to date as you draw.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["3d","solid","model","ductwork","elevation","bottom of duct","tag","glb","coordination"],"headings":[{"id":"the-same-run-in-3d","text":"The same run in 3D","level":2},{"id":"building-them-again","text":"Building them again","level":2},{"id":"without-the-add-on","text":"Without the add-on","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-run-in-3d\"\u003EThe same run in 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach duct run is built as one closed solid on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab, at its elevation (the bottom of the duct), with its elbows, transitions and branches. It is redrawn in the same step whenever the run changes, so undo takes the 2D and the 3D back together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe solids carry the tag \u003Ccode\u003EHVAC ductwork\u003C/code\u003E: hide or show them all from the 3D tab&#039;s tags. Each system has its own color.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"building-them-again\"\u003EBuilding them again\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCT3D\u003C/code\u003E builds every run&#039;s solid again and removes solids whose run is gone. Use it after opening a drawing that was edited without the HVAC add-on (its runs moved, but their solids did not), or after switching solids back on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch solids off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHVAC\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids on the 3D tab\u003C/span\u003E for very large drawings; the solids already there stay until you erase them on the 3D tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-the-add-on\"\u003EWithout the add-on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA solid is an ordinary 3D group. It saves with the drawing (DWG and DXF), shows on the 3D tab and exports to GLB, OBJ and STL with everything else, with or without HVAC switched on.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The same run in 3D Each duct run is built as one closed solid on the 3D tab, at its elevation (the bottom of the duct), with its elbows, transitions and branches. It is redrawn in the same step whenever the run changes, so undo takes the 2D and the 3D back together. The solids carry the tag HVAC ductwork: hide or show them all from the 3D tab's tags. Each system has its own color. Building them again DUCT3D builds every run's solid again and removes solids whose run is gone. Use it after opening a drawing that was edited without the HVAC add-on (its runs moved, but their solids did not), or after switching solids back on. Switch solids off in Settings › CadCraft › HVAC › Solids on the 3D tab for very large drawings; the solids already there stay until you erase them on the 3D tab. Without the add-on A solid is an ordinary 3D group. It saves with the drawing (DWG and DXF), shows on the 3D tab and exports to GLB, OBJ and STL with everything else, with or without HVAC switched on."},{"slug":"hvac-takeoff","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: ducts in TakeoffCraft","summary":"Send the duct schedule to TakeoffCraft as items — a PDF of the plan with the takeoff inside, or the assemblies as a pack for your price library.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["takeoffcraft","takeoff","estimate","quantities","items","pack","price library","sheet metal","pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"a-pdf-with-the-takeoff-inside","text":"A PDF with the takeoff inside","level":2},{"id":"the-assemblies-as-a-pack","text":"The assemblies as a pack","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pdf-with-the-takeoff-inside\"\u003EA PDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDucts for TakeoffCraft (PDF with the takeoff inside)\u003C/span\u003E plots the plan on one sheet (ARCH D or ISO A1, at the largest standard scale that fits) and puts a TakeoffCraft takeoff inside the PDF. Open the PDF in TakeoffCraft and it offers the takeoff:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eone \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear item\u003C/strong\u003E per duct shape, size and material, with a mark along every straight piece;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eone \u003Cstrong\u003Ecount item\u003C/strong\u003E per fitting and size, with a mark where each one sits;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach item&#039;s assembly has its \u003Cstrong\u003Esheet metal\u003C/strong\u003E: the duct&#039;s perimeter × L, or the fitting&#039;s area × N.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft measures the marks at the sheet&#039;s scale, so its lengths are the schedule&#039;s lengths, and you can see on the sheet what each one is. The scale is set and checked against the longest duct piece. Items have no rates: a rate is yours to set.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-assemblies-as-a-pack\"\u003EThe assemblies as a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuct assemblies for TakeoffCraft (pack)\u003C/span\u003E writes the same items without quantities as a TakeoffCraft pack (a \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file). In TakeoffCraft open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a pack file\u003C/span\u003E to add them to your price library. Each item notes the quantity it had in the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A PDF with the takeoff inside EXPORT › Ducts for TakeoffCraft (PDF with the takeoff inside) plots the plan on one sheet (ARCH D or ISO A1, at the largest standard scale that fits) and puts a TakeoffCraft takeoff inside the PDF. Open the PDF in TakeoffCraft and it offers the takeoff: one linear item per duct shape, size and material, with a mark along every straight piece; one count item per fitting and size, with a mark where each one sits; each item's assembly has its sheet metal: the duct's perimeter × L, or the fitting's area × N. TakeoffCraft measures the marks at the sheet's scale, so its lengths are the schedule's lengths, and you can see on the sheet what each one is. The scale is set and checked against the longest duct piece. Items have no rates: a rate is yours to set. The assemblies as a pack EXPORT › Duct assemblies for TakeoffCraft (pack) writes the same items without quantities as a TakeoffCraft pack (a .json file). In TakeoffCraft open Library › Trade packs › Open a pack file to add them to your price library. Each item notes the quantity it had in the drawing."},{"slug":"hvac-sizing","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: sizing ducts","summary":"Equal-friction duct sizing from the airflow, the formulas the add-on uses and where they come from, and the duct calculator.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["sizing","equal friction","friction rate","airflow","cfm","velocity","colebrook","darcy","equivalent diameter","huebscher","flat oval","ashrae","calculator"],"headings":[{"id":"equal-friction","text":"Equal friction","level":2},{"id":"the-formulas","text":"The formulas","level":2},{"id":"the-duct-calculator","text":"The duct calculator","level":2},{"id":"what-sizing-does-not-do","text":"What sizing does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"equal-friction\"\u003EEqual friction\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery section is sized for the same pressure loss per length — the friction rate. Set the rate in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHVAC\u003C/span\u003E: 0.1 in. w.g. per 100 ft or 0.8 Pa/m to start (a common starting rate, not a rule). Give a section its airflow (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAirflow\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTSIZE\u003C/code\u003E) and the add-on:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efinds the round diameter that loses exactly that rate at that airflow;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Erounds it \u003Cstrong\u003Eup\u003C/strong\u003E to a stocked size, so the duct loses no more than the rate: round ducts in 1 in steps to 10 in, then 2 in steps; metric 63, 80, 100, 125, 160, 200, 250, 315, 355, 400 mm and on;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efor a rectangular or flat oval duct, keeps the height you set (12 in or 300 mm unless you change it) and widens it in 2 in or 50 mm steps until its equivalent diameter is at least the round one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt then says the size, the velocity and the friction rate it really has. A rectangular duct with sides more than 4 to 1 is flagged.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-formulas\"\u003EThe formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe method and figures are the public ones of the duct design chapter of the ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals (2021):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFriction loss (Darcy–Weisbach): Δp/L = (f / D) × ρV²/2.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe friction factor from the Colebrook equation: 1/√f = −2 log₁₀(ε / 3.7D + 2.51 / (Re √f)), solved by iteration.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStandard air: density 1.204 kg/m³ and kinematic viscosity 1.51 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s (20 °C, sea level); Re = V × D / ν.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERoughness: smooth 0.03 mm (aluminum, PVC, stainless), medium-smooth 0.09 mm (galvanized steel), medium-rough 0.9 mm (fibrous glass duct board), rough 3.0 mm (flexible duct, fully extended). Choose the material in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERectangular ducts: the circular equivalent for equal friction and airflow, Huebscher (1948): De = 1.30 (a × b)^0.625 / (a + b)^0.25.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlat oval ducts: Heyt and Diaz (1975): De = 1.55 A^0.625 / P^0.25, with A = πb²/4 + b(a − b) and P = πb + 2(a − b).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWorked examples the add-on is tested against: 1000 cfm in a 12 in round duct moves at 1273 fpm and loses 0.189 in. w.g. per 100 ft; 24 × 12 in is equivalent to 18.3 in round; 1000 cfm at 0.1 in. w.g. per 100 ft needs 13.66 in exactly, so 14 in round or 14 × 12 in rectangular.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-duct-calculator\"\u003EThe duct calculator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTCALC\u003C/code\u003E asks for an airflow and a friction rate and lists the exact round diameter, then the stocked round, rectangular and flat oval sizes with their velocity and friction. It changes nothing in the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-sizing-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat sizing does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt does not add up fitting losses, balance branches, or check the fan: it sizes each section on its own by friction rate. Check velocities against your project&#039;s noise limits.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Equal friction Every section is sized for the same pressure loss per length — the friction rate. Set the rate in Settings › CadCraft › HVAC: 0.1 in. w.g. per 100 ft or 0.8 Pa/m to start (a common starting rate, not a rule). Give a section its airflow (DUCT › Airflow, or DUCTSIZE) and the add-on: finds the round diameter that loses exactly that rate at that airflow; rounds it up to a stocked size, so the duct loses no more than the rate: round ducts in 1 in steps to 10 in, then 2 in steps; metric 63, 80, 100, 125, 160, 200, 250, 315, 355, 400 mm and on; for a rectangular or flat oval duct, keeps the height you set (12 in or 300 mm unless you change it) and widens it in 2 in or 50 mm steps until its equivalent diameter is at least the round one. It then says the size, the velocity and the friction rate it really has. A rectangular duct with sides more than 4 to 1 is flagged. The formulas The method and figures are the public ones of the duct design chapter of the ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals (2021): Friction loss (Darcy–Weisbach): Δp/L = (f / D) × ρV²/2. The friction factor from the Colebrook equation: 1/√f = −2 log₁₀(ε / 3.7D + 2.51 / (Re √f)), solved by iteration. Standard air: density 1.204 kg/m³ and kinematic viscosity 1.51 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s (20 °C, sea level); Re = V × D / ν. Roughness: smooth 0.03 mm (aluminum, PVC, stainless), medium-smooth 0.09 mm (galvanized steel), medium-rough 0.9 mm (fibrous glass duct board), rough 3.0 mm (flexible duct, fully extended). Choose the material in Settings or Properties. Rectangular ducts: the circular equivalent for equal friction and airflow, Huebscher (1948): De = 1.30 (a × b)^0.625 / (a + b)^0.25. Flat oval ducts: Heyt and Diaz (1975): De = 1.55 A^0.625 / P^0.25, with A = πb²/4 + b(a − b) and P = πb + 2(a − b). Worked examples the add-on is tested against: 1000 cfm in a 12 in round duct moves at 1273 fpm and loses 0.189 in. w.g. per 100 ft; 24 × 12 in is equivalent to 18.3 in round; 1000 cfm at 0.1 in. w.g. per 100 ft needs 13.66 in exactly, so 14 in round or 14 × 12 in rectangular. The duct calculator DUCTCALC asks for an airflow and a friction rate and lists the exact round diameter, then the stocked round, rectangular and flat oval sizes with their velocity and friction. It changes nothing in the drawing. What sizing does not do It does not add up fitting losses, balance branches, or check the fan: it sizes each section on its own by friction rate. Check velocities against your project's noise limits."},{"slug":"hvac-network","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: pressure in the whole duct network","summary":"DUCTNET follows the drawn ducts from the fan, finds the pressure lost to every free end, the critical path, the damper each other end needs and the fan pressure.","section":"reference","order":25,"keywords":["network","pressure","critical path","fan pressure","damper","balancing","fitting loss","DUCTNET","DNET","static pressure","system"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTSIZE\u003C/code\u003E sizes one run. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTNET\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDNET\u003C/code\u003E) looks at the whole drawn system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive each section its airflow (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAirflow\u003C/span\u003E of the duct, in cfm or L/s). The free end of a run takes the airflow of the section at that end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUCTNET\u003C/code\u003E and click the end of the run where the fan or air handler connects. Ends that touch another run are joined; branches are found where one run ends on another.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt prints each free end with its airflow and the pressure lost on the way from the fan, the \u003Cstrong\u003Ecritical path\u003C/strong\u003E (the end that loses most), the \u003Cstrong\u003Edamper\u003C/strong\u003E each other end needs to balance, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Efan pressure\u003C/strong\u003E: the critical path plus the pressure of coils and filters you type with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E option (from the makers&#039; data).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFriction comes from the same Colebrook formula as the sizing. Fittings (elbows, the turn where a size changes, branch takeoffs) use a short list of rounded default coefficients: they are labelled defaults, not a fitting database, and stack effect is ignored. Check the result with your engineer before relying on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESections that are too small for their airflow, too fast or too flat are listed by run. Loops are ignored (the calculation follows a tree) and said. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMethod\u003C/span\u003E switches the size check between equal friction and a velocity limit. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › Duct network pressure writes every section to CSV.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DUCTSIZE sizes one run. DUCTNET (DNET) looks at the whole drawn system. Give each section its airflow (the Airflow of the duct, in cfm or L/s). The free end of a run takes the airflow of the section at that end. Type DUCTNET and click the end of the run where the fan or air handler connects. Ends that touch another run are joined; branches are found where one run ends on another. It prints each free end with its airflow and the pressure lost on the way from the fan, the critical path (the end that loses most), the damper each other end needs to balance, and the fan pressure: the critical path plus the pressure of coils and filters you type with the Equipment option (from the makers' data). Friction comes from the same Colebrook formula as the sizing. Fittings (elbows, the turn where a size changes, branch takeoffs) use a short list of rounded default coefficients: they are labelled defaults, not a fitting database, and stack effect is ignored. Check the result with your engineer before relying on it. Sections that are too small for their airflow, too fast or too flat are listed by run. Loops are ignored (the calculation follows a tree) and said. Method switches the size check between equal friction and a velocity limit. Export › Duct network pressure writes every section to CSV."},{"slug":"hvac-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:hvac","title":"HVAC: not built yet","summary":"What the HVAC add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","roadmap","hvac"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERisers and drops:\u003C/strong\u003E a run stays at one elevation; draw a vertical run as separate runs at their own elevations.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFitting pressure losses\u003C/strong\u003E, static pressure totals, branch balancing and fan selection: sizing is by friction rate only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAir terminals\u003C/strong\u003E (diffusers, grilles, registers), dampers, fire dampers, access doors and equipment: draw them as blocks for now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDuct gauges, reinforcement and hangers\u003C/strong\u003E by construction standard, and insulation or lining thickness.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBranches that follow their main\u003C/strong\u003E when the main moves: draw the branch to the main again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESplitting a run\u003C/strong\u003E at a point, or \u003Cstrong\u003Ejoining\u003C/strong\u003E two runs into one: draw it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDucts on sheets\u003C/strong\u003E other than through viewports, and \u003Cstrong\u003Esections and elevations\u003C/strong\u003E cut from the 3D ductwork.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpanish words\u003C/strong\u003E for the add-on: they come with the next language update.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet Risers and drops: a run stays at one elevation; draw a vertical run as separate runs at their own elevations. Fitting pressure losses, static pressure totals, branch balancing and fan selection: sizing is by friction rate only. Air terminals (diffusers, grilles, registers), dampers, fire dampers, access doors and equipment: draw them as blocks for now. Duct gauges, reinforcement and hangers by construction standard, and insulation or lining thickness. Branches that follow their main when the main moves: draw the branch to the main again. Splitting a run at a point, or joining two runs into one: draw it again. Ducts on sheets other than through viewports, and sections and elevations cut from the 3D ductwork. Spanish words for the add-on: they come with the next language update."}]};
